+# CSV import
+
+## What and why
+
+Sales keeps leads in spreadsheets and wants to load a whole CSV file into contactbook
+instead of adding contacts one by one. The import applies every good row, skips bad ones,
+and returns a report of what it created, what it updated, and what went wrong, per row,
+so the file can be fixed and imported again. The file has the same columns as our CSV
+export (`name,email,phone,tags`).
+
+An import **merges** into existing contacts: it never deletes a contact, never removes a
+tag and never wipes a stored phone (see `docs/adr/0001-csv-import-merges.md`).
+
+## Interface (fixed by the requester, verbatim)
+
+- `src/contactbook/importer.py`: `import_contacts(repo: InMemoryContactRepository, data: str | bytes) -> ImportReport`
+  where `data` is the file content: raw bytes as read from disk, or already-decoded text.
+- `ImportReport` (dataclass, same module): `created: list[str]`, `updated: list[str]` (normalised
+  emails), `errors: list[RowIssue]`, `warnings: list[RowIssue]`.
+- `RowIssue` (dataclass, same module): `row: int`, `column: str | None`, `message: str`.
+- `ImportFileError(ContactError)` in `src/contactbook/errors.py`, raised when the file as a whole
+  can't be imported.
+
+`RowIssue.column` is the lower-case column name `name`, `email`, `phone` or `tags`, or `None`
+when the issue isn't about one of those.
+
+## Acceptance criteria
+
+### Decoding
+
+1. `bytes` input is decoded as UTF-8. A leading UTF-8 BOM is ignored.
+2. `str` input that starts with the BOM character U+FEFF is treated as though it weren't there.
+3. Bytes that aren't valid UTF-8 → `ImportFileError` whose message tells the user to re-save
+   the file as "CSV UTF-8". The repository is unchanged.
+
+### File-level checks (each raises `ImportFileError`, repository unchanged)
+
+4. A file that is empty, or contains only whitespace and/or a BOM, is rejected.
+5. Row 1 is the header. Header cells are matched ignoring case and surrounding spaces, in any
+   order.
+6. Required columns: `email`, plus either `name` or both `first_name` and `last_name`.
+   A file missing either one → rejected.
+7. `phone` and `tags` columns are optional.
+8. A recognised column (`name`, `email`, `phone`, `tags`, `first_name`, `last_name`) that
+   appears twice in the header → rejected. *(my call)*
+9. A file with more than 1000 contact rows → rejected, and nothing is imported. Blank rows
+   (see 12) are not counted. Rows that turn out to be errors are counted. Exactly 1000 is
+   accepted.
+10. CSV the parser can't read (e.g. an unterminated quoted cell) → rejected. *(my call)*
+11. A file with only a header row → an empty report (all four lists empty), no exception.
+
+### Rows
+
+12. Blank lines, and rows whose cells are all empty or whitespace, are skipped silently. They
+    produce no report entry.
+13. `RowIssue.row` is the file's line number where the row starts: the header is row 1, the
+    first contact is row 2, and every line counts, blank lines included.
+14. The delimiter is a comma, with standard CSV quoting (as written by `export_csv`).
+    Tags are separated by `;` inside the `tags` cell. Each tag is trimmed, and empty pieces
+    (`"vip;"`, an empty cell) are ignored.
+15. Header columns that aren't recognised are ignored. Each one yields one warning:
+    `row=1`, `column=None`, and a message that names the column. They never cause an error.
+    Header cells that are empty (spreadsheets add trailing commas) are ignored without a
+    warning. *(my call)*
+16. If the header has `name` as well as `first_name`/`last_name`, `name` is used and the pair
+    is ignored, with warnings as in 15. *(my call)*
+17. With `first_name` and `last_name`, the name is the two joined by one space, then
+    normalised like any name (so a blank last name gives just the first name). A problem
+    with that name is reported with `column="name"`.
+18. A row with more cells than the header → an error with `column=None`. Its cells aren't
+    checked further, and its email doesn't count for criterion 21. A row with fewer
+    cells than the header → the missing cells count as blank. *(my call)*
+19. All stored values are normalised exactly as `Contact.create` would normalise them.
+    Report emails are the normalised emails.
+
+### Errors (the row is skipped, not applied at all)
+
+20. An invalid name, email or tag → an error on that row, with `column` set to `name`, `email`
+    or `tags`. Each invalid field gives one error. A row with an error is not applied,
+    not even partly to an existing contact. Such a row gets no phone warning.
+21. An email that already appeared in an earlier row of the same file (compared
+    case-insensitively after normalisation, whatever happened to that earlier row) → an error
+    with `column="email"` on each later row. Only the first row with that email can be
+    applied.
+22. Good rows are imported even when other rows have errors (partial import).
+
+### Phone (a warning, never an error)
+
+23. An invalid phone → a warning with `column="phone"`. The rest of the row is applied
+    (created or merged), and the phone is left as it was (no phone for a new contact).
+24. A blank phone cell, or no `phone` column at all, leaves a stored phone untouched.
+
+### Create vs merge
+
+25. A row whose email isn't in the repository creates a contact. Its email is appended to
+    `created`.
+26. A row whose email is already in the repository (case-insensitively) is merged into
+    that contact:
+    - the name is replaced by the file's name;
+    - the phone is replaced only by a valid, non-blank phone cell;
+    - the file's tags are added to the stored tags, and no tag is ever removed.
+27. If the merge changes the stored contact, its email is appended to `updated`. If it
+    changes nothing, the email appears in neither list. *(my call)*
+28. `created`, `updated`, `errors` and `warnings` are in file order. Each list keeps its
+    entries in ascending row order, and the header warnings come first.
+
+## Non-goals
+
+- Wiring into `ContactBook`, the HTTP API or a CLI.
+- Encodings other than UTF-8, guessing the encoding, and delimiters other than comma.
+- Deleting contacts, removing tags, or clearing phones through an import.
+- Dry run / preview mode, all-or-nothing mode.
+- Thread safety (the repository isn't thread-safe either).
+
+## Scope limits
+
+- New module `src/contactbook/importer.py`, new `ImportFileError` in `errors.py`, exports in
+  `__init__.py`, tests in `tests/test_importer.py`. No changes to existing behaviour.
+- Standard library only.
